ADDART({id:"thylacoleo",t:"Thylacoleo, the Marsupial Lion",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦁",w:"Thylacoleo",r:["diprotodon","megalania","thylacine","australiacountry","iceagemammals","smilodon"],
p:[["A pouched predator","Australia’s marsupial lion lived until about 40,000 years ago."],["The strongest bite","For its size, it had one of the most powerful bites of any mammal."],["A climbing hunter","It may have dragged prey up into trees."]],
l:"Thylacoleo carnifex, the marsupial lion, was a meat-eating marsupial that lived in Australia until about 40,000 years ago, during the Pleistocene. Despite its name, it was not related to lions; its closest living relatives are wombats and koalas, which descended from plant-eating ancestors. About the size of a leopard, roughly 1.5 meters long and weighing around 100 to 130 kilograms, it was the largest meat-eating mammal in Australia at the time. Instead of long canine teeth, Thylacoleo had enormous blade-like cheek teeth that sliced like shears, and large incisors at the front for stabbing. Studies of its skull suggest it had one of the strongest bites, relative to body size, of any mammal ever. Its front legs were very powerful, with a huge, semi-opposable thumb claw that it probably used to grab and kill prey such as giant kangaroos and young Diprotodon. Scratch marks in caves show that it could climb, and it may have carried prey into trees like a leopard, or climbed to raise its young. Thylacoleo disappeared at about the time the first people spread across Australia, along with other megafauna.",
s:[["Discovery","Richard Owen named Thylacoleo in 1859 from a skull. Its strange teeth puzzled scientists; some first thought it ate plants. A nearly complete skeleton found in the Nullarbor caves in 2002 showed its full body."],["Bite force","A 2005 study compared the bite strength of living and extinct mammals for their size and found Thylacoleo’s was the highest, stronger relative to its size than a lion’s."],["A climber","Scratch marks on the walls of Tight Entrance Cave in Western Australia, studied in 2016, suggest Thylacoleo climbed up steep rock, perhaps to reach dens where it raised young."],["Extinction","Thylacoleo disappeared around 40,000 years ago, along with many large Australian animals. Scientists continue to debate the roles of people, fire and climate change."]],
k:[["Lived","Until about 40,000 years ago"],["Where","Australia"],["Size","About 1.5 m long, 100–130 kg"],["Relatives","Wombats and koalas"],["Famous for","Extremely strong bite"],["Special claw","Large thumb claw"]],
d:[["1859","Thylacoleo named by Richard Owen"],["2002","Complete skeletons found in Nullarbor caves"],["2005","Study ranks its bite as the strongest for its size"],["2016","Cave scratch marks studied"]],
s2:[["Not a lion","Thylacoleo means ‘pouch lion’, but it was a marsupial, more closely related to a koala than to any cat."],["Rock art","Some researchers think an ancient Aboriginal rock painting in the Kimberley region may show Thylacoleo, though this is debated."]],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that carries its young in a pouch"],["Megafauna","Very large animals"],["Incisor","A front cutting tooth"],["Pleistocene","The epoch of the ice ages"]],
f:["Thylacoleo was a marsupial.","It had a very strong bite.","It lived in Australia."],
fun:"The marsupial lion was more closely related to koalas than to lions."});

ADDART({id:"cavelion",t:"The Cave Lion",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦁",w:"Cave lion",r:["cavebear","mammoth","caveart","smilodon","iceagemammals","woollyrhino"],
p:[["An Ice Age lion","Larger than today’s lions."],["In cave paintings","Shown in Chauvet Cave in France."],["Frozen cubs","Perfectly preserved cubs found in Siberia."]],
l:"The cave lion (Panthera spelaea) was a large lion that lived across Europe, northern Asia and northwestern North America during the last ice age, from about 370,000 years ago until it became extinct around 13,000 years ago. It was about 5 to 10 percent larger than modern lions, with males possibly weighing up to around 300 kilograms. Despite its name, it lived mainly on the open cold grasslands known as the mammoth steppe, hunting reindeer, horses, deer and young bison; it is called the cave lion because many of its bones were found in caves, where it may have hunted hibernating cave bears. Genetic studies show it was a separate species from modern lions, having split from them about 500,000 years ago. Unlike African male lions, cave lions probably had little or no mane, judging from Ice Age cave art, which depicts them in detail, especially in Chauvet Cave in France, painted more than 30,000 years ago. In 2015, 2017 and 2018, remarkably well-preserved frozen cave lion cubs, with fur, whiskers and even eyelashes, were discovered in the permafrost of Siberia. Cave lions may have died out due to climate change, loss of prey and human hunting.",
s:[["In art","Cave lions are among the most frequently drawn animals in Ice Age art. At Chauvet Cave, a panel shows a group of lions hunting bison. An ivory figurine of a lion-headed human, the ‘Lion Man’ from Germany, is about 40,000 years old."],["Frozen cubs","Two cubs named Uyan and Dina were found in 2015 in Yakutia, Russia. A cub called Sparta, found in 2018, is about 28,000 years old and so well preserved that scientists could study its fur."],["No mane?","Cave paintings show male lions without large manes, though some have a slight ruff. Scientists think the cold climate and different social life may explain this."],["Extinction","Cave lions disappeared around 13,000 years ago, about the same time as other Ice Age animals such as woolly mammoths in much of their range."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Panthera spelaea"],["Lived","About 370,000–13,000 years ago"],["Where","Europe, northern Asia, Alaska and Yukon"],["Size","About 5–10% larger than modern lions"],["Prey","Reindeer, horses, deer, bison"],["Famous art","Chauvet Cave, France"]],
d:[["1810","Cave lion described from fossils in Germany"],["1994","Chauvet Cave paintings discovered"],["2015","Frozen cubs Uyan and Dina found in Siberia"],["2018","Frozen cub Sparta found"]],
s2:[["Lion Man","The Lion Man of Hohlenstein-Stadel, carved from mammoth ivory about 40,000 years ago, is one of the oldest known sculptures."],["Relatives","A related lion, the American lion, lived farther south in North America and was even larger."]],
g:[["Mammoth steppe","A vast cold grassland of the ice age"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"],["Species","A particular kind of living thing"],["Figurine","A small sculpture"]],
f:["Cave lions lived during the ice age.","Cave lions appear in Chauvet Cave art.","Frozen cave lion cubs were found in Siberia."],
fun:"A frozen cave lion cub found in Siberia still had its whiskers after about 28,000 years."});

ADDART({id:"arsinoitherium",t:"Arsinoitherium",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Arsinoitherium",r:["paraceratherium","rhino","elephant","egypt","fossils","andrewsarchus"],
p:[["Two giant horns","Huge paired horns grew side by side on its snout."],["Not a rhino","Its closest relatives were elephants and sea cows."],["Ancient Egypt’s swamps","Fossils come from the Fayum in Egypt."]],
l:"Arsinoitherium was a large plant-eating mammal that lived in northern and eastern Africa and the Arabian Peninsula about 36 to 27 million years ago, during the late Eocene and early Oligocene. It looked rather like a rhinoceros, about 3 meters long and 1.8 meters tall at the shoulder, but its most striking feature was a pair of enormous, hollow, cone-shaped horns growing side by side from the top of its snout, with a smaller pair behind them. Unlike rhino horns, which are made of keratin, Arsinoitherium’s horns were made of bone. Despite its appearance, it was not related to rhinos: it belonged to an extinct group called the embrithopods, which are most closely related to elephants, sea cows and hyraxes, part of a group of mammals that evolved in Africa. Its teeth suggest it ate tough leaves, and its legs and feet suggest it may have lived in swampy, forested areas, perhaps spending time in water like a hippopotamus. The best fossils come from the Fayum Depression in Egypt, a famous fossil site that was once a lush, swampy coastal area near the ancient Tethys Sea. It was named after Arsinoe II, a queen of ancient Egypt.",
s:[["Discovery","Arsinoitherium was described in 1902 by Hugh Beadnell from fossils found in the Fayum, Egypt. Its name honors Queen Arsinoe II, whose palace once stood near the fossil site."],["The horns","The horns were hollow and grew from the nasal bones. They may have been used for display, to push rivals or to clear vegetation. Some scientists think they also helped amplify calls."],["Elephant relatives","Embrithopods belong to the Afrotheria, a group of mammals that includes elephants, sea cows, hyraxes, aardvarks and tenrecs, all of which evolved in Africa when it was an island continent."],["The Fayum","The Fayum Depression west of Cairo has produced fossils of early elephants, primates, whales and birds. Nearby Wadi Al-Hitan, the ‘Valley of the Whales’, is a World Heritage Site."]],
k:[["Lived","About 36–27 million years ago"],["Where","Africa and Arabia"],["Length","About 3 meters"],["Horns","Paired, made of bone"],["Relatives","Elephants, sea cows, hyraxes"],["Named after","Queen Arsinoe II of Egypt"]],
d:[["1902","Arsinoitherium described from the Fayum"],["2004","Fossils found in Ethiopia"]],
s2:[["Convergent look","Arsinoitherium’s rhino-like body is an example of unrelated animals evolving similar shapes."],["Big teeth","It had 44 teeth, the full set found in early placental mammals."]],
g:[["Embrithopod","An extinct group of horned mammals related to elephants"],["Afrotheria","A group of mammals that evolved in Africa"],["Eocene","The epoch from about 56 to 34 million years ago"],["Fayum","A fossil-rich area in Egypt"]],
f:["Arsinoitherium had two huge horns.","It was related to elephants.","Its fossils come from Egypt."],
fun:"Arsinoitherium looked like a rhinoceros but was a distant relative of elephants."});

ADDART({id:"ambulocetus",t:"Ambulocetus, the Walking Whale",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐋",w:"Ambulocetus",r:["whale","basilosaurus","evolution","fossils","croc","tiktaalik"],
p:[["A whale with legs","An early whale that could walk and swim."],["Living like a crocodile","It ambushed prey at the water’s edge."],["Evidence for evolution","It shows how whales moved from land to sea."]],
l:"Ambulocetus natans, whose name means ‘walking and swimming whale’, was an early relative of whales that lived about 49 to 48 million years ago, in the Eocene, in what is now Pakistan. It was about 3 meters long and weighed perhaps 140 to 300 kilograms, and looked rather like a crocodile with fur, with a long snout, sharp teeth, short legs and large webbed feet. Ambulocetus could walk on land, though clumsily, and swam by paddling with its large hind feet and moving its body and tail up and down, as otters and whales do, rather than side to side like crocodiles. It probably lived in shallow coastal waters and estuaries, lurking in the water to ambush fish and land animals that came to drink. Ambulocetus is one of several fossils that reveal how whales evolved from land-dwelling mammals related to hippos over about 10 million years. Earlier relatives such as Pakicetus lived mainly on land; Ambulocetus was amphibious; later forms such as Rodhocetus and Basilosaurus became increasingly adapted to water, until fully marine whales lost their hind legs. Chemical studies of its teeth show that it drank both fresh and salt water.",
s:[["Discovery","Ambulocetus was found in 1992 by a team led by Hans Thewissen in the Kuldana Formation of northern Pakistan and described in 1994. It became famous as a ‘missing link’ in whale evolution."],["How we know it’s a whale","Ambulocetus’s skull and ear bones share special features with whales, including a thick, dense ear bone called an involucrum that helps hear underwater."],["From land to sea","The whale family tree includes Pakicetus (about 50 million years ago, mostly on land), Ambulocetus (amphibious), Rodhocetus (more aquatic), and Basilosaurus and Dorudon (fully aquatic, with tiny hind legs)."],["Hippo relatives","DNA and fossil evidence show that whales are most closely related to hippos among living animals. Both evolved from a common ancestor among even-toed hoofed mammals."]],
k:[["Lived","About 49–48 million years ago"],["Where","Pakistan"],["Length","About 3 meters"],["Lifestyle","Amphibious ambush predator"],["Name means","Walking and swimming whale"],["Closest living relatives of whales","Hippos"]],
d:[["1983","Pakicetus described"],["1992","Ambulocetus found"],["1994","Ambulocetus described"],["2001","Ankle bones link whales to hippos"]],
s2:[["Back legs inside","Some modern whales still have tiny leftover hip bones inside their bodies, a reminder of their four-legged ancestors."],["Up and down","Whales and dolphins swim by moving their tails up and down, like running mammals, while fish and crocodiles swim side to side."]],
g:[["Amphibious","Living both on land and in water"],["Ambush","A surprise attack"],["Ear bone","A bone involved in hearing"],["Missing link","A fossil showing a transition between groups"]],
f:["Ambulocetus could walk and swim.","It was an early whale.","Whales are related to hippos."],
fun:"Ambulocetus was a whale ancestor that walked on four legs."});

ADDART({id:"leedsichthys",t:"Leedsichthys",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Leedsichthys",r:["fishes","shark","whale","megalodon","pliosaurus","fossils"],
p:[["A giant filter feeder","Possibly the largest bony fish ever."],["Ate plankton","Like a whale shark, it strained tiny food from water."],["Jurassic seas","It lived about 165 to 152 million years ago."]],
l:"Leedsichthys problematicus was an enormous fish that lived in the seas of the Middle and Late Jurassic, about 165 to 152 million years ago, with fossils found in England, France, Germany and Chile. It is thought to be the largest bony fish that ever lived. Early estimates suggested lengths of 25 to 30 meters, but a careful study in 2013 concluded it probably reached about 16.5 meters long, still as long as a large whale shark, the biggest fish alive today. Like whale sharks and baleen whales, Leedsichthys was a filter feeder: it swam slowly with its huge mouth open, straining tiny animals such as shrimp, jellyfish and fish larvae from the water using thousands of fine, comb-like structures on its gills called gill rakers. Leedsichthys is difficult to study because its skeleton was made largely of cartilage that did not fossilize well, so only fragments are usually found, which is why its scientific name means ‘problematic’. It was named after Alfred Leeds, an English farmer and fossil collector who found its remains near Peterborough in the 1880s. Bite marks on some fossils show that marine reptiles such as pliosaurs hunted it.",
s:[["Discovery","Alfred Nicholson Leeds collected fossils from clay pits near Peterborough, England. Leedsichthys was named in 1889. Its bones were so strange and fragmentary that scientists at first argued about what kind of animal it was."],["How big?","Because fossils are incomplete and the fish kept growing through life, size estimates have varied widely. Growth rings in its bones suggest it took about 20 to 40 years to reach full size."],["Filter feeding","Its gill rakers, delicate bony combs lined with tiny teeth, sieved plankton from huge volumes of water, much as baleen whales do today."],["Predators","Fossils show bite marks from marine reptiles such as Metriorhynchus and pliosaurs, which probably attacked young, injured or dead Leedsichthys."]],
k:[["Lived","About 165–152 million years ago"],["Where","Seas of Europe and South America"],["Length","Probably about 16.5 meters"],["Diet","Plankton (filter feeder)"],["Record","Largest known bony fish"],["Named after","Alfred Leeds"]],
d:[["1886","First remains found near Peterborough"],["1889","Leedsichthys named"],["2013","Study revises its length to about 16.5 m"]],
s2:[["Bony fish","Most fish alive today are bony fish. Sharks and rays, including the whale shark, have skeletons made of cartilage."],["Modern giants","The ocean sunfish is the heaviest bony fish alive today, weighing over 2 tonnes."]],
g:[["Filter feeder","An animal that strains food from water"],["Gill rakers","Comb-like structures on gills"],["Plankton","Tiny drifting living things"],["Cartilage","Firm, flexible tissue"]],
f:["Leedsichthys was a filter feeder.","It may be the largest bony fish ever.","It lived in the Jurassic."],
fun:"Leedsichthys’s name means ‘problematic’ because its fossils are so hard to piece together."});

ADDART({id:"megaloceros",t:"The Giant Deer (Irish Elk)",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦌",w:"Megaloceros",r:["iceagemammals","mammoth","cavelion","caveart","woollyrhino","deer"],
p:[["Huge antlers","Up to 3.6 meters across."],["Not just Irish","It lived across Europe and Asia."],["Not an elk","It was a giant deer, related to fallow deer."]],
l:"The giant deer, Megaloceros giganteus, often called the Irish elk, was one of the largest deer that ever lived. It roamed across Europe and Asia, from Ireland to Siberia, from about 400,000 years ago until about 7,700 years ago. Males stood about 2.1 meters tall at the shoulder and weighed up to around 700 kilograms, and they carried the largest antlers of any known deer, spreading up to 3.6 meters from tip to tip and weighing up to about 40 kilograms. Like modern deer, males grew a new set of antlers each year and shed them after the breeding season, which required a lot of calcium and energy. Despite its common name, it was neither only Irish nor an elk: many well-preserved skeletons were found in Irish peat bogs and lake sediments, but its closest living relative is the fallow deer. Giant deer grazed on open grasslands and light woodland. They disappeared from Ireland and much of Europe at the end of the last ice age as forests spread and grasslands shrank, but small populations survived in western Siberia until about 7,700 years ago, overlapping with early humans who hunted them.",
s:[["Antlers","Scientists have debated whether the huge antlers were mainly for fighting rivals or for display. Studies suggest they were used for both, with males showing off and sometimes locking antlers in contests."],["Irish bogs","Hundreds of giant deer skeletons have been found in Ireland, preserved in lake mud and bogs. Mounted skeletons were displayed in grand houses and museums from the 1600s."],["Extinction","As the climate warmed after the ice age, nutritious grasslands were replaced by forests. Growing enormous antlers each year required rich food, so giant deer may have struggled. Human hunting probably added pressure."],["A famous debate","In the 1800s and 1900s, the giant deer was used in arguments about evolution; some claimed its antlers grew so large they caused its extinction, an idea scientists later rejected."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Megaloceros giganteus"],["Lived","About 400,000–7,700 years ago"],["Where","Europe and Asia"],["Antler span","Up to about 3.6 meters"],["Shoulder height","About 2.1 meters"],["Closest relative","Fallow deer"]],
d:[["1695","Giant deer antlers described from Ireland"],["1799","Megaloceros named"],["2004","Study shows it survived in Siberia until about 7,700 years ago"]],
s2:[["Cave art","Giant deer appear in Ice Age cave paintings, such as at Lascaux in France, sometimes with a dark hump on the shoulders."],["Shedding antlers","Like modern deer, male giant deer dropped their antlers every year and grew new ones, an enormous task given their size."]],
g:[["Antler","A bony branched growth shed each year by deer"],["Peat bog","A wetland where dead plants build up as peat"],["Extinction","When a species dies out"],["Grassland","Land mostly covered in grasses"]],
f:["The giant deer had huge antlers.","It was not only Irish.","Its closest relative is the fallow deer."],
fun:"The giant deer’s antlers could be wider than a car is long."});

ADDART({id:"ransomware",t:"Ransomware",c:"Technology",i:"🔒",w:"Ransomware",r:["malware","hacking","phishing","crypto","twofactor","cryptocurrency"],
p:[["Files held hostage","Malware that locks data and demands payment."],["Hospitals and cities hit","Attacks can stop vital services."],["Backups protect","Keeping safe copies helps victims recover."]],
l:"Ransomware is a type of malicious software that locks or encrypts a victim’s files or computer systems, making them unusable, and then demands a ransom payment, usually in cryptocurrency, to restore access. Many attackers also steal data first and threaten to publish it if the ransom is not paid, a tactic called double extortion. Ransomware usually gets in through phishing emails, stolen or weak passwords, or unpatched security flaws in software. It has hit businesses, schools, hospitals, local governments, pipelines and shipping companies, sometimes causing serious disruption: hospitals have had to cancel operations, and in 2021 an attack on the Colonial Pipeline in the United States caused fuel shortages along the East Coast. Many attacks are carried out by criminal groups that sell or rent their ransomware to others, called ransomware-as-a-service. Law enforcement agencies from many countries have worked together to take down major gangs. Security experts and governments generally advise victims not to pay, because payment funds crime and does not guarantee recovery. The best protections are regular backups kept offline, prompt software updates, strong passwords with two-factor authentication and staff training to spot phishing.",
s:[["How it works","Once inside a network, attackers often spend days exploring and disabling backups before launching the encryption. A ransom note appears explaining how to pay, often with a deadline."],["WannaCry","In May 2017, the WannaCry ransomware spread worldwide in hours, infecting hundreds of thousands of computers in about 150 countries, including the UK’s National Health Service, using a stolen hacking tool and an unpatched Windows flaw."],["Fighting back","In 2024, an international police operation called Operation Cronos disrupted LockBit, one of the most prolific ransomware gangs. Free decryption tools for some ransomware are shared by the No More Ransom project."],["Protection","Keep software updated, use strong, unique passwords and two-factor authentication, be careful with email attachments and links, and keep regular backups disconnected from the network."]],
k:[["What it does","Locks files and demands payment"],["Common entry","Phishing, weak passwords, unpatched software"],["Payment demanded in","Usually cryptocurrency"],["Famous attack","WannaCry (2017)"],["Double extortion","Stealing data and threatening to leak it"],["Best defense","Offline backups and updates"]],
d:[["1989","The ‘AIDS Trojan’, an early ransomware, spreads on floppy disks"],["2013","CryptoLocker spreads widely"],["2017","WannaCry attack"],["2021","Colonial Pipeline attack"],["2024","Operation Cronos disrupts LockBit"]],
s2:[["No More Ransom","Launched in 2016 by police and security companies, the No More Ransom website offers free tools to unlock files from some ransomware."],["Kill switch","WannaCry was slowed when a British researcher, Marcus Hutchins, registered a web domain that acted as a hidden ‘kill switch’."]],
g:[["Ransomware","Malware that demands payment to unlock files"],["Encrypt","To scramble data so it cannot be read without a key"],["Ransom","Money demanded to release something"],["Backup","A spare copy of data"]],
f:["Ransomware locks files and demands money.","WannaCry spread worldwide in 2017.","Offline backups help victims recover."],
fun:"The WannaCry attack was slowed when a researcher accidentally triggered its hidden ‘kill switch’."});

ADDART({id:"vpns",t:"VPNs",c:"Technology",i:"🛡️",w:"Virtual private network",r:["internet","crypto","dataprivacy","wifi","ipaddresses","webcookies"],
p:[["A private tunnel","A VPN encrypts traffic between your device and a server."],["Hides your address","Websites see the VPN’s address, not yours."],["Not total privacy","VPNs protect some things but not everything."]],
l:"A virtual private network, or VPN, is a service that creates an encrypted connection, often described as a tunnel, between a device and a server run by the VPN provider or an organization. Internet traffic passes through this tunnel, so that people on the same network, such as public Wi-Fi in a café, and the internet service provider cannot easily see what websites are being visited or read the data. Websites see the VPN server’s internet address instead of the user’s real one, which can make the user appear to be in another location. VPNs were first developed for businesses, letting employees connect securely to their company’s private network from home or while traveling, and this remains a major use. Many people now use consumer VPN services to protect privacy on public Wi-Fi, to reduce tracking, and to get around geographic restrictions on streaming services or government censorship. However, VPNs do not make users anonymous: the VPN company itself can see their traffic, websites can still track them with cookies and accounts, and VPNs do not protect against malware or phishing. Some countries restrict or ban VPN use.",
s:[["How it works","A VPN app on a phone or computer encrypts data before it leaves the device and sends it to the VPN server, which decrypts it and passes it on to the website. Replies travel back the same way."],["Business VPNs","Companies use VPNs so remote employees can safely reach internal files and systems. During the COVID-19 pandemic, use of business VPNs grew enormously as people worked from home."],["Limits","Choosing a trustworthy provider matters, because it can see users’ traffic. Free VPNs may sell data or show ads. Browsing over secure HTTPS connections already encrypts the content of most web pages."],["Rules","Some countries, including China, Russia and Iran, restrict VPNs, allowing only government-approved services. In other countries, people use VPNs to reach news and social media that are blocked."]],
k:[["Stands for","Virtual private network"],["Main job","Encrypting traffic in a tunnel"],["Hides","Your IP address from websites"],["Common uses","Remote work, public Wi-Fi, privacy"],["Does not","Make you fully anonymous"],["Restricted in","Some countries"]],
d:[["1996","Microsoft engineers develop an early VPN protocol, PPTP"],["2015","WireGuard VPN protocol begins development"],["2020","Business VPN use surges during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["HTTPS","The padlock in a browser’s address bar shows an HTTPS connection, which encrypts traffic between your browser and that website even without a VPN."],["Streaming","Many people use VPNs to watch shows available in other countries, though this often breaks the streaming service’s rules."]],
g:[["VPN","A service that sends traffic through an encrypted tunnel"],["Encryption","Scrambling data so only the right people can read it"],["IP address","A number identifying a device on the internet"],["Censorship","Blocking information or communication"]],
f:["A VPN encrypts internet traffic.","VPNs hide your IP address from websites.","VPNs do not make you completely anonymous."],
fun:"When you use a VPN, a website might think you are in another country."});

ADDART({id:"nfts",t:"NFTs",c:"Technology",i:"🖼️",w:"Non-fungible token",r:["cryptocurrency","creatoreconomy","copyrightonline","lootboxes","pixelart","memes"],
p:[["Digital certificates of ownership","Records on a blockchain linked to digital items."],["A 2021 boom","Some sold for millions of dollars."],["A big crash","Most lost most of their value by 2023."]],
l:"An NFT, or non-fungible token, is a unique digital record stored on a blockchain, a shared public ledger, that is used to show ownership of a particular item, often a digital image, video, music file, game item or collectible. ‘Non-fungible’ means each token is unique and cannot be swapped one-for-one like money or bitcoin. Buying an NFT usually does not mean owning the copyright to the artwork or even a copy of the file itself; the token typically contains a link to where the file is stored. NFTs existed from about 2014, but they exploded in popularity in 2021, when collections such as CryptoPunks and the Bored Ape Yacht Club sold for huge sums, and in March 2021 the digital artist Beeple sold an NFT artwork, Everydays: The First 5000 Days, at Christie’s auction house for about 69 million US dollars. Supporters said NFTs let digital artists sell work directly and earn royalties on resales. Critics pointed to scams, market manipulation, theft and the energy used by some blockchains. The market crashed sharply in 2022, and studies found that most NFTs had lost almost all their value by 2023, though some artists and games still use them.",
s:[["How they work","An NFT is created, or ‘minted’, on a blockchain such as Ethereum. The blockchain records who owns each token and every time it is sold, and smart contracts can pay the original creator a royalty on resales."],["The boom","In 2021, NFT sales reached billions of dollars. Celebrities bought cartoon apes, and brands launched NFT collections. Beeple’s sale made him one of the most valuable living artists at auction."],["The crash","As cryptocurrency prices fell in 2022, NFT trading collapsed. A 2023 study found that most NFT collections had little or no trading value left."],["Energy","Ethereum, the main NFT blockchain, switched in September 2022 to a system called proof of stake, cutting its energy use by about 99.9 percent and answering one major criticism."]],
k:[["Stands for","Non-fungible token"],["Stored on","A blockchain"],["Usually represents","Digital art, collectibles, game items"],["Does not usually include","Copyright"],["Famous sale","Beeple, about $69 million (2021)"],["Market crash","2022"]],
d:[["2014","An early NFT, Quantum, is minted"],["2017","CryptoPunks and CryptoKitties launch"],["2021","Beeple’s $69 million sale at Christie’s"],["2022","NFT market crashes; Ethereum switches to proof of stake"]],
s2:[["Fungible","Money is fungible: one dollar can be swapped for any other. An NFT is like a signed original print: each one is different."],["Right-click","Critics joked that anyone could ‘right-click and save’ the images linked to many NFTs, highlighting that buyers owned a token rather than the picture itself."]],
g:[["NFT","A unique token recording ownership on a blockchain"],["Blockchain","A shared digital ledger"],["Mint","To create an NFT"],["Royalty","A payment to a creator when work is resold"]],
f:["NFT stands for non-fungible token.","Buying an NFT usually does not give you copyright.","The NFT market crashed in 2022."],
fun:"A single NFT artwork by Beeple sold for about 69 million dollars in 2021."});

ADDART({id:"firewalls",t:"Firewalls",c:"Technology",i:"🧱",w:"Firewall (computing)",r:["malware","hacking","internet","ipaddresses","vpns","ransomware"],
p:[["A security guard for networks","Firewalls filter traffic in and out."],["Rules decide","They block or allow connections by rules."],["Everywhere","In routers, computers and company networks."]],
l:"A firewall is a security system that monitors and controls network traffic coming into and going out of a computer or network, based on a set of security rules. Its name comes from the fireproof walls in buildings that stop fire spreading from one area to another. A firewall acts like a security guard at a gate, checking each piece of data trying to pass and deciding whether to allow or block it, based on where it comes from, where it is going and what kind of traffic it is. Firewalls can be hardware devices, such as the one built into most home internet routers, or software programs running on computers, such as the firewalls included in Windows and macOS. Early firewalls, developed in the late 1980s, simply filtered individual packets of data by address and port number. Later ‘stateful’ firewalls tracked whole conversations, and modern ‘next-generation’ firewalls can inspect content, recognize applications and detect known attacks. Firewalls are a key part of network security, blocking many attacks from the internet and stopping some malware from sending data out, but they cannot stop everything, such as phishing emails that trick users, so they are used together with other protections.",
s:[["Packets and ports","Data travels across networks in small packets, each labeled with addresses and port numbers that identify the service, such as web browsing or email. Firewalls examine these labels to decide what to allow."],["Home firewalls","Most home routers block unsolicited incoming connections by default, protecting devices from many attacks. Computers also have built-in software firewalls that should normally be left switched on."],["Company firewalls","Organizations use powerful firewalls to divide networks into zones, protect servers and enforce rules about which websites and services employees can use."],["National firewalls","Some governments use large-scale filtering, such as China’s system often called the ‘Great Firewall’, to block foreign websites and control information."]],
k:[["Purpose","Filtering network traffic"],["Named after","Fireproof walls in buildings"],["Types","Hardware and software"],["Early firewalls","Late 1980s"],["Modern type","Next-generation firewalls"],["Limits","Cannot stop phishing or all malware"]],
d:[["1988","Early packet-filtering firewalls developed at DEC"],["1990s","Stateful firewalls developed"],["2004","Windows XP turns on its firewall by default"]],
s2:[["Not a cure-all","A firewall is one layer of protection; security experts recommend ‘defense in depth’, using many layers."],["Great Firewall","The term ‘Great Firewall of China’ is a play on the Great Wall of China."]],
g:[["Firewall","A system that filters network traffic"],["Packet","A small unit of data sent over a network"],["Port","A number identifying a type of network service"],["Router","A device that connects networks"]],
f:["Firewalls filter network traffic.","Most home routers include a firewall.","Firewalls cannot stop every attack."],
fun:"Computer firewalls are named after the fireproof walls that stop fires spreading in buildings."});

ADDART({id:"pixelart",t:"Pixel Art",c:"Technology",i:"👾",w:"Pixel art",r:["videogames","arcadegames","screens","emoji","gameconsoles"],
p:[["Pictures made of tiny squares","Each pixel is placed by hand."],["From early video games","Born from the limits of old computers."],["Still popular","Loved in indie games and art today."]],
l:"Pixel art is a style of digital art in which images are created and edited at the level of individual pixels, the tiny squares of color that make up a digital image. Instead of painting with smooth brushstrokes, pixel artists deliberately place each pixel, often working with a small, limited color palette and low resolution. The style grew out of necessity in the 1970s and 1980s, when early computers, arcade machines and home game consoles had very little memory and could display only small images with a few colors. Artists working on games such as Space Invaders, Pac-Man, Super Mario Bros. and The Legend of Zelda had to create recognizable characters from grids sometimes just 8 or 16 pixels wide, using clever tricks such as dithering, alternating pixels of two colors to suggest a third. As technology improved, games moved to detailed 3D graphics, but pixel art never disappeared. From the 2000s, independent game developers revived it in hits such as Minecraft’s blocky textures, Stardew Valley and Celeste, valuing its charm, clarity and nostalgia. Today pixel art is also used for icons, animations, illustrations, emoji, fan art and even large real-world mosaics.",
s:[["Limits as inspiration","Early systems like the Nintendo Entertainment System (1983) could show only a few colors per small tile, forcing artists to design simple, bold shapes. Mario’s mustache and cap were partly designed so his face would be readable in few pixels."],["Techniques","Pixel artists use outlines, careful shading, dithering and anti-aliasing by hand. Animators create movement frame by frame, such as a character’s walk cycle in just a few frames."],["Indie revival","Small game studios often choose pixel art because it is charming, works well on any screen and can be made by small teams. Games such as Undertale and Stardew Valley were largely made by single developers."],["Beyond games","Large collaborative projects, such as Reddit’s r/place in 2017, 2022 and 2023, let millions of people place one pixel at a time on a shared canvas, creating huge pixel artworks."]],
k:[["Definition","Art made by placing individual pixels"],["Origins","1970s–1980s computers and games"],["Typical features","Low resolution, limited palette"],["Technique","Dithering"],["Indie favorites","Stardew Valley, Celeste, Undertale"],["Collaborative project","Reddit’s r/place"]],
d:[["1978","Space Invaders released"],["1980","Pac-Man released"],["1985","Super Mario Bros. released"],["2016","Stardew Valley released"],["2017","First r/place canvas on Reddit"]],
s2:[["Space Invaders","The aliens in Space Invaders (1978) are among the most recognizable pixel characters ever made, and have inspired street art around the world."],["Pixel","The word pixel is short for ‘picture element’."]],
g:[["Pixel","A tiny square of color in a digital image"],["Palette","A set of colors used in an artwork"],["Dithering","Mixing pixels of two colors to suggest another"],["Resolution","How many pixels make up an image"]],
f:["Pixel art is made from individual pixels.","It began with early video games.","Many indie games use pixel art."],
fun:"Mario has a mustache partly because it was easier to show than a mouth with so few pixels."});

ADDART({id:"opendata",t:"Open Data",c:"Technology",i:"📂",w:"Open data",r:["databases","apis","internet","stats","dataprivacy","wikipedia"],
p:[["Data anyone can use","Freely available for anyone to use and share."],["From governments and science","Weather, transport, maps and research."],["Powering apps","Many apps are built on open data."]],
l:"Open data is data that is freely available for anyone to access, use, change and share, for any purpose, usually requiring only that the source is credited. Governments, scientific organizations, international bodies and some companies publish open data on topics such as weather, public transport timetables, maps, budgets and spending, census results, health statistics, pollution, crime, satellite images and scientific research. Making data open aims to increase transparency and accountability in government, encourage innovation and help researchers, journalists and citizens understand the world. For example, transport apps that show when the next bus will arrive often use open data published by transit agencies; weather apps rely on open data from national weather services; and OpenStreetMap, a free map of the world, is built by volunteers and released as open data. Many open datasets are published with licenses such as Creative Commons and accessed through websites and APIs. The ‘open data movement’ grew in the late 2000s, with governments launching portals such as data.gov in the United States in 2009 and data.gov.uk in 2010. Open data must be balanced with privacy, so personal information is removed or protected.",
s:[["What makes data open?","Open data should be available free of charge online, in formats that computers can easily read, such as spreadsheets rather than scanned pages, and with a license allowing anyone to reuse it."],["Examples","The European Union’s Copernicus program releases free satellite images of Earth. NASA shares data from its missions. The World Bank publishes development statistics for every country."],["OpenStreetMap","Started in 2004, OpenStreetMap has been built by millions of volunteers who map roads, buildings and paths. It is used by humanitarian groups after disasters and by many apps and websites."],["Privacy","Open data is usually about places, things or groups rather than identifiable people. Data such as health records is anonymized or kept closed to protect privacy."]],
k:[["Definition","Data free for anyone to use and share"],["Publishers","Governments, scientists, international bodies"],["Examples","Weather, transport, maps, budgets"],["Famous project","OpenStreetMap (2004)"],["Government portals","data.gov (2009), data.gov.uk (2010)"],["Balance with","Privacy"]],
d:[["2004","OpenStreetMap founded"],["2009","US launches data.gov"],["2010","UK launches data.gov.uk"],["2013","G8 Open Data Charter signed"]],
s2:[["Citizen science","Volunteers contribute open data by counting birds, measuring rainfall or classifying galaxies in online projects."],["Hackathons","Developers often build apps from open data at ‘hackathons’, short events where people collaborate on projects."]],
g:[["Open data","Data anyone can freely use"],["License","Permission saying how something may be used"],["Transparency","Openness about what is done"],["Anonymize","Remove information that identifies people"]],
f:["Open data is free for anyone to use.","Transport apps often use open data.","OpenStreetMap is built by volunteers."],
fun:"OpenStreetMap, a free map of the world, has been drawn by millions of volunteers."});

ADDART({id:"refraction",t:"Refraction and Lenses",c:"Science",i:"🔍",w:"Refraction",r:["light","mirrors","eye","eyeglasses","rainbows","camera"],
p:[["Light bends","Light changes direction when it enters a new material."],["Bent straws","A straw looks broken in a glass of water."],["Lenses focus light","Used in glasses, cameras and telescopes."]],
l:"Refraction is the bending of light as it passes from one transparent material into another, such as from air into water or glass. It happens because light travels at different speeds in different materials: about 300,000 kilometers per second in a vacuum, but about 225,000 in water and 200,000 in glass. When light enters a slower material at an angle, one side of the beam slows down first, causing the beam to change direction, much as a cart turns if one wheel hits sand before the other. Refraction explains many everyday sights: a straw in a glass of water looks bent; swimming pools look shallower than they are; and stars twinkle as their light passes through moving layers of air. Different colors of light bend by slightly different amounts, which is how a prism splits white light into a spectrum and how raindrops create rainbows. Lenses are curved pieces of glass or plastic that use refraction to focus or spread light. Convex lenses, thicker in the middle, bring light together and can magnify; concave lenses, thinner in the middle, spread light out. Lenses are used in eyeglasses, contact lenses, cameras, microscopes, telescopes and projectors, and the eye itself has a lens.",
s:[["Snell’s law","The amount light bends depends on the materials and the angle. The relationship is described by Snell’s law, named after the Dutch scientist Willebrord Snellius (1621), though the Persian scientist Ibn Sahl described it around 984."],["Index of refraction","A material’s refractive index tells how much it slows light. Water’s is about 1.33, glass about 1.5 and diamond about 2.42, which is why diamonds sparkle so brilliantly."],["Mirages","On hot days, air near the ground is hotter and less dense, bending light upward. This can make the sky appear reflected on a road like a pool of water, a mirage."],["Lenses in the eye","The cornea and lens of the eye refract light to focus images on the retina. Eyeglasses correct vision when the eye focuses light in front of or behind the retina."]],
k:[["Refraction","Bending of light between materials"],["Cause","Light changes speed"],["Speed in glass","About 200,000 km/s"],["Convex lens","Focuses light, magnifies"],["Concave lens","Spreads light out"],["Diamond’s refractive index","About 2.42"]],
d:[["c. 984","Ibn Sahl describes the law of refraction"],["c. 1286","Eyeglasses invented in Italy"],["1608","First telescopes made in the Netherlands"],["1621","Snell formulates his law"]],
s2:[["Fish eyes","Spearfishers must aim below where a fish appears, because refraction makes it look higher than it really is."],["Burning glass","A magnifying glass can focus sunlight onto a tiny spot hot enough to start a fire."]],
g:[["Refraction","The bending of light"],["Lens","A curved piece of glass or plastic that bends light"],["Convex","Curving outward"],["Concave","Curving inward"]],
f:["Light bends when it changes speed.","A straw looks bent in water because of refraction.","Convex lenses focus light."],
fun:"Swimming pools look shallower than they really are because of refraction."});

ADDART({id:"heattransfer",t:"Conduction, Convection and Radiation",c:"Science",i:"♨️",w:"Heat transfer",r:["temperature","thermo","entropy","sun","climate","absolutezero"],
p:[["Heat on the move","Heat flows from hot to cold in three ways."],["Touching, flowing, shining","Conduction, convection and radiation."],["Everyday science","From cooking to keeping warm."]],
l:"Heat is energy that flows from hotter objects to colder ones, and it moves in three main ways: conduction, convection and radiation. Conduction is the transfer of heat through direct contact, as faster-vibrating particles bump into their neighbors and pass on energy. It happens best in solids, especially metals, which are good conductors; that is why a metal spoon in hot soup quickly becomes hot. Materials that conduct heat poorly, such as wood, plastic, wool, air and foam, are insulators. Convection is the transfer of heat by the movement of liquids and gases: warm fluid becomes less dense and rises, while cooler, denser fluid sinks, creating circulating currents. Convection heats a pot of water from the bottom, carries warm air around a room and drives winds, ocean currents and the slow flow of rock in Earth’s mantle. Radiation is the transfer of heat by electromagnetic waves, mainly infrared, which can travel through empty space; this is how the Sun’s heat reaches Earth and how you feel warmth from a fire across a room. Understanding heat transfer helps engineers design houses, clothing, engines, cooking equipment and spacecraft.",
s:[["Conduction","Metals conduct heat well because they have free electrons that carry energy. Pots have metal bottoms to conduct heat from the stove and plastic or wooden handles to stay cool."],["Convection","Hot-air balloons rise because heated air is less dense. Sea breezes happen when land heats faster than water in the day, so warm air rises over land and cooler air flows in from the sea."],["Radiation","All objects give off infrared radiation; hotter objects give off more. Thermal cameras detect it, showing warm people and animals glowing in the dark."],["Keeping heat in or out","A vacuum flask reduces all three: the vacuum between its walls stops conduction and convection, and shiny surfaces reflect radiation. Houses use insulation, double glazing and reflective materials."]],
k:[["Conduction","Through direct contact"],["Convection","By moving fluids"],["Radiation","By electromagnetic waves"],["Good conductors","Metals"],["Insulators","Wood, plastic, wool, air"],["Sun’s heat reaches Earth by","Radiation"]],
d:[["1701","Isaac Newton describes his law of cooling"],["1800","William Herschel discovers infrared radiation"],["1892","James Dewar invents the vacuum flask"]],
s2:[["Penguin huddles","Emperor penguins huddle together to reduce heat loss, taking turns on the cold outside of the group."],["Blankets don’t make heat","Blankets keep you warm by trapping air, an insulator, which slows the loss of your own body heat."]],
g:[["Conduction","Heat transfer through contact"],["Convection","Heat transfer by moving fluid"],["Radiation","Heat transfer by waves"],["Insulator","A material that slows heat flow"]],
f:["Heat flows from hot to cold.","Metals are good conductors.","The Sun’s heat reaches Earth by radiation."],
fun:"A blanket doesn’t make heat; it traps air to keep your own warmth in."});

ADDART({id:"absolutezero",t:"Absolute Zero",c:"Science",i:"❄️",w:"Absolute zero",r:["temperature","thermo","superconduct","entropy","plasma","atom"],
p:[["The coldest possible","−273.15 °C, or 0 kelvin."],["Can never quite be reached","Scientists get within billionths of a degree."],["Strange things happen","Matter behaves in unusual quantum ways."]],
l:"Absolute zero is the lowest possible temperature, at which particles have the least possible energy. It is defined as 0 kelvin, equal to −273.15 degrees Celsius or −459.67 degrees Fahrenheit. Temperature measures how fast atoms and molecules move and vibrate, so as something cools, its particles slow down; at absolute zero, they would have only the tiny minimum motion allowed by quantum physics, called zero-point energy. The laws of thermodynamics say absolute zero can never actually be reached, because each step of cooling removes a smaller amount of heat. But scientists have come extraordinarily close, cooling atoms to less than a billionth of a degree above absolute zero using lasers and magnetic traps. At such low temperatures, matter behaves strangely. Some metals and ceramics become superconductors, carrying electricity with no resistance; liquid helium becomes a superfluid that can flow without friction and creep up the walls of its container; and clouds of atoms can merge into a single quantum state called a Bose–Einstein condensate. The coldest natural place known is the Boomerang Nebula, about 1 kelvin, colder than the background temperature of space, about 2.7 kelvin.",
s:[["The Kelvin scale","The British scientist William Thomson, Lord Kelvin, proposed an absolute temperature scale in 1848. Kelvins have the same size as Celsius degrees but start at absolute zero, so water freezes at 273.15 K."],["Laser cooling","Scientists slow atoms by hitting them with carefully tuned laser beams that push against their motion, like rolling balls into a headwind. This won the 1997 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Bose–Einstein condensates","Predicted in the 1920s by Satyendra Nath Bose and Albert Einstein, this new state of matter was first made in 1995 by Eric Cornell and Carl Wieman. In 2018, NASA’s Cold Atom Lab created them aboard the International Space Station."],["Superconductors","Superconductivity, discovered in 1911 by Heike Kamerlingh Onnes in mercury cooled to about 4 K, is used in MRI scanners and particle accelerators."]],
k:[["Value","0 K = −273.15 °C = −459.67 °F"],["Meaning","Lowest possible energy"],["Can it be reached?","No, only approached"],["Record cooling","Less than a billionth of a degree above 0 K"],["Strange states","Superfluids, Bose–Einstein condensates"],["Space background","About 2.7 K"]],
d:[["1848","Lord Kelvin proposes the absolute scale"],["1908","Helium first liquefied"],["1911","Superconductivity discovered"],["1995","First Bose–Einstein condensate"],["2018","Cold Atom Lab on the ISS"]],
s2:[["Cold space","The Boomerang Nebula, at about 1 K, is the coldest natural place known in the universe."],["Superfluid fountains","Superfluid helium can climb up the sides of a container and spurt out like a fountain."]],
g:[["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature"],["Kelvin","The scientific unit of temperature"],["Superfluid","A fluid that flows without friction"],["Bose–Einstein condensate","A state of matter near absolute zero"]],
f:["Absolute zero is −273.15 °C.","Absolute zero cannot be reached.","Some materials become superconductors when very cold."],
fun:"Laboratories on Earth have made things colder than anywhere in outer space."});

ADDART({id:"alloys",t:"Alloys",c:"Science",i:"⚙️",w:"Alloy",r:["metals","atom","plastics","gems","corrosion"],
p:[["Mixed metals","An alloy combines a metal with other elements."],["Stronger and better","Alloys can be harder or rust-proof."],["Bronze to steel","Alloys shaped human history."]],
l:"An alloy is a material made by combining a metal with one or more other elements, usually other metals or carbon, to create a substance with improved properties. Pure metals are often too soft, weak or prone to corrosion for many uses, but mixing them can make materials that are stronger, harder, lighter, more resistant to rust or better at conducting heat or electricity. Alloys have shaped human history. Bronze, a mixture of copper and tin, was the first important alloy, giving its name to the Bronze Age, which began around 3300 BCE; it was harder than copper and was used for tools, weapons and statues. Steel, iron mixed with a small amount of carbon, is far stronger than pure iron and became the backbone of the Industrial Revolution and modern construction. Stainless steel adds chromium, which forms a thin protective layer that stops rust. Brass, copper and zinc, is used for musical instruments and fittings. Aluminum alloys are light and strong, ideal for aircraft and cars, while titanium alloys are used in jet engines, spacecraft and medical implants. Even gold jewelry is usually an alloy, mixed with other metals to make it harder.",
s:[["Why mixing helps","In a pure metal, atoms are arranged in neat layers that slide over each other easily, making the metal soft. Adding atoms of a different size disrupts the layers, making them harder to slide and the alloy stronger."],["Steel","Steel usually contains less than 2 percent carbon. In 1856, Henry Bessemer’s process made steel cheap to produce in large amounts, transforming railways, ships, bridges and skyscrapers."],["Everyday alloys","Coins are often alloys such as cupronickel. Solder, used to join electronic parts, was traditionally tin and lead and is now often tin with silver and copper. Pewter is mostly tin."],["Smart alloys","Shape-memory alloys such as nitinol, a nickel-titanium alloy, return to their original shape when heated. They are used in medical stents, eyeglass frames and robotics."]],
k:[["Definition","A metal mixed with other elements"],["First important alloy","Bronze (copper + tin)"],["Steel","Iron + carbon"],["Stainless steel","Steel + chromium"],["Brass","Copper + zinc"],["Light alloys","Aluminum and titanium alloys"]],
d:[["c. 3300 BCE","Bronze Age begins"],["c. 1200 BCE","Iron working spreads"],["1856","Bessemer process for steel"],["1913","Stainless steel developed by Harry Brearley"]],
s2:[["Karat gold","24-karat gold is pure; 18-karat gold is 75 percent gold mixed with other metals to make it stronger."],["Damascus steel","Damascus steel blades, made in the Middle East centuries ago, were famous for their wavy patterns and toughness."]],
g:[["Alloy","A mixture of a metal with other elements"],["Bronze","An alloy of copper and tin"],["Steel","An alloy of iron and carbon"],["Corrosion","Damage to metal, such as rust"]],
f:["Bronze is made of copper and tin.","Steel is iron mixed with carbon.","Stainless steel resists rust."],
fun:"Most gold jewelry is not pure gold; it is an alloy made harder with other metals."});

ADDART({id:"averages",t:"Mean, Median and Mode",c:"Mathematics",i:"📊",w:"Average",r:["stats","prob","percent","graphs","esports","money"],
p:[["Three kinds of average","Mean, median and mode describe the middle of data."],["Each tells a different story","They can give very different answers."],["Everyday use","Weather, sports, wages and grades."]],
l:"An average is a single number that represents a typical value in a set of data. There are three common types. The mean, what most people call the average, is found by adding all the values and dividing by how many there are; the mean of 2, 3 and 7 is 12 ÷ 3 = 4. The median is the middle value when the data is arranged in order; for an even number of values, it is halfway between the two middle ones. The mode is the value that appears most often. Each average is useful in different situations. The mean uses every value but can be pulled up or down by a few extreme values, called outliers. For example, if nine people earn 30,000 a year and one earns 3 million, the mean income is 327,000, which describes nobody, while the median of 30,000 is much more typical. That is why statistics on incomes and house prices often use the median. The mode is useful for things like the most common shoe size or most popular choice in a survey. Along with measures of spread, such as the range, averages help summarize data in science, sports, weather, economics and everyday life.",
s:[["Mean","The mean is used for things like average temperatures, test scores and rainfall. A batting average in cricket or baseball is a kind of mean."],["Median","Because it is not affected by extreme values, the median is often used for incomes, house prices and waiting times. Half the values are above it and half below."],["Mode","The mode can be used with categories, such as favorite colors, where a mean or median makes no sense. Data can have more than one mode or none."],["Misleading averages","Saying ‘the average family has 2.4 children’ uses the mean, though no family has 2.4 children. Choosing which average to report can make data look very different, so it is worth asking which one is used."]],
k:[["Mean","Sum ÷ number of values"],["Median","The middle value"],["Mode","The most common value"],["Affected by outliers","Mean"],["Used for incomes","Often the median"],["Works with categories","Mode"]],
s2:[["Spread","Two data sets can have the same average but very different spreads. The range, the difference between the largest and smallest values, is a simple measure of spread."],["Bill Gates walks into a bar","A classic example: when a billionaire walks into a café, the mean wealth of the customers soars, but the median barely changes."]],
g:[["Mean","The sum of values divided by their number"],["Median","The middle value in ordered data"],["Mode","The most frequent value"],["Outlier","A value far from the others"]],
f:["The mean is the sum divided by the count.","The median is the middle value.","The mode is the most common value."],
fun:"When a billionaire walks into a café, the mean wealth of the customers soars but the median barely changes."});

ADDART({id:"electromagnetism",t:"Electromagnetism",c:"Science",i:"🧲",w:"Electromagnetism",r:["magnet","elec","faraday","light","motors"],
p:[["Electricity and magnetism are linked","Moving charges make magnetic fields."],["Electromagnets","Coils of wire become magnets when current flows."],["Light is electromagnetic","Light is a wave of electric and magnetic fields."]],
l:"Electromagnetism is the branch of physics that studies electricity and magnetism and the deep connection between them. For centuries they were thought to be separate, but in 1820 the Danish scientist Hans Christian Ørsted noticed that an electric current flowing through a wire made a nearby compass needle move, showing that electricity produces magnetism. In 1831 the English scientist Michael Faraday discovered the reverse: moving a magnet near a coil of wire produces an electric current, a process called electromagnetic induction. In the 1860s the Scottish physicist James Clerk Maxwell combined these discoveries into a set of equations describing electric and magnetic fields, and showed that changing fields could travel through space as waves at the speed of light, concluding that light itself is an electromagnetic wave. This led to the discovery of radio waves and the whole electromagnetic spectrum, from radio waves and microwaves to infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays. Electromagnetism is one of the four fundamental forces of nature and underlies most modern technology: electromagnets, electric motors, generators, transformers, loudspeakers, MRI scanners, radio, television, mobile phones and Wi-Fi all depend on it.",
s:[["Electromagnets","Wrapping wire around an iron core and passing current through it makes an electromagnet, which can be switched on and off. Large ones lift scrap metal; small ones are in doorbells, speakers and hard drives."],["Motors and generators","Electric motors use magnetic forces on currents to make things spin. Generators do the opposite, spinning coils in magnetic fields to produce electricity, as in power stations and wind turbines."],["Maxwell’s equations","Maxwell’s four equations, published in 1865, are among the greatest achievements of physics. Albert Einstein kept a picture of Maxwell, along with Faraday and Newton, on his study wall."],["Radio waves","In 1887, Heinrich Hertz produced and detected radio waves in his laboratory, confirming Maxwell’s prediction. Guglielmo Marconi later used them for wireless communication."]],
k:[["Studies","Electricity and magnetism together"],["Key discovery (1820)","Current moves a compass (Ørsted)"],["Induction (1831)","Michael Faraday"],["Unified theory (1865)","James Clerk Maxwell"],["Light is","An electromagnetic wave"],["Uses","Motors, generators, radio, MRI"]],
d:[["1820","Ørsted discovers electromagnetism"],["1831","Faraday discovers induction"],["1865","Maxwell publishes his equations"],["1887","Hertz detects radio waves"]],
s2:[["Fundamental force","Electromagnetism is one of four fundamental forces, along with gravity and the strong and weak nuclear forces. It holds atoms and molecules together."],["Maglev trains","Magnetic levitation trains use powerful electromagnets to float above the track and glide at high speeds."]],
g:[["Electromagnetism","The link between electricity and magnetism"],["Induction","Making current by changing magnetic fields"],["Electromagnet","A magnet made by electric current"],["Electromagnetic spectrum","The full range of electromagnetic waves"]],
f:["Electric currents create magnetic fields.","Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction.","Light is an electromagnetic wave."],
fun:"A compass needle twitching near a wire in 1820 revealed that electricity and magnetism are connected."});

ADDART({id:"tinyhomes",t:"Tiny Homes",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏠",w:"Tiny-house movement",r:["housing","architecture","sdgs","digitalnomads","urban"],
p:[["Living small","Homes often under about 40 square meters."],["Cheaper and simpler","Lower costs and fewer belongings."],["Rules and limits","Zoning laws can make them hard to place."]],
l:"Tiny homes are very small houses, often defined as under about 37 to 40 square meters (400 square feet), designed to make the most of limited space. Many are built on trailers so they can be moved, while others sit on foundations, in backyards or in small communities. The tiny-house movement grew in the United States in the late 1990s and 2000s, and spread to countries such as Canada, Australia, New Zealand, the United Kingdom, Germany and Japan, especially after the 2008 financial crisis, as people looked for ways to reduce housing costs, avoid debt, live more simply and lower their environmental impact. Tiny homes use clever design: lofts for sleeping, foldaway tables, stairs with storage drawers, multi-use furniture and compact kitchens and bathrooms. Supporters say they cost much less to build, heat and maintain, encourage owning fewer possessions and can provide affordable housing, including villages of tiny homes for people experiencing homelessness. Challenges include building codes and zoning rules that set minimum house sizes or restrict where tiny homes can be parked, difficulty getting loans, and the realities of living in very small spaces, especially for families.",
s:[["Design tricks","Sleeping lofts, hidden storage, fold-down beds, sliding doors and large windows make tiny homes feel bigger. Many use composting toilets, solar panels and rainwater collection."],["Costs","Tiny homes usually cost a fraction of a standard house, though prices vary widely. Owners also need land or a place to park, which can be expensive or hard to find."],["Rules","Many places require homes to have a minimum floor area or be connected to utilities. Some cities have changed rules to allow tiny homes or small backyard dwellings to increase housing supply."],["Homes for homeless people","Some cities and charities have built villages of tiny homes to give people experiencing homelessness private, secure shelter as a step toward permanent housing."]],
k:[["Typical size","Under about 37–40 m²"],["Types","On wheels or foundations"],["Movement grew","Late 1990s–2000s"],["Benefits","Lower cost, smaller footprint"],["Challenges","Zoning, building codes, loans"],["Features","Lofts, hidden storage, multi-use furniture"]],
d:[["1854","Henry David Thoreau publishes Walden, about simple living in a small cabin"],["1999","Jay Shafer starts a tiny house company"],["2008","Financial crisis boosts interest"]],
s2:[["Thoreau’s cabin","Henry David Thoreau’s cabin at Walden Pond, where he lived from 1845 to 1847, was about 14 square meters."],["Capsule hotels","Japan’s capsule hotels and small micro-apartments in cities such as Hong Kong and Tokyo show other ways people live in very small spaces."]],
g:[["Tiny home","A very small house"],["Zoning","Rules about how land can be used"],["Building code","Rules for how buildings must be built"],["Minimalism","Living with fewer possessions"]],
f:["Tiny homes are very small houses.","Many tiny homes are built on trailers.","Zoning rules can make tiny homes hard to place."],
fun:"Henry David Thoreau’s famous cabin at Walden Pond was about the size of a small bedroom."});

ADDART({id:"climatemigration",t:"Climate Migration",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🌍",w:"Climate migration",r:["migration","climchange","sealevel","droughts","atolls"],
p:[["Moving because of climate","Floods, storms, drought and rising seas force people to move."],["Record disaster displacement","About 46 million displacements in 2024."],["Mostly within countries","Most people move inside their own country."]],
l:"Climate migration refers to the movement of people driven in part by the effects of climate change and environmental change, such as floods, storms, wildfires, droughts, heat, rising sea levels, coastal erosion and the loss of land for farming. Some movements are sudden, when people flee disasters like cyclones and floods, and many return home afterward; others are gradual, as slow changes like drought, desertification or saltwater entering farmland make it harder to make a living. Most climate-related movement happens within countries, often from rural areas to cities, rather than across borders. According to the Internal Displacement Monitoring Centre, disasters caused about 45.8 million internal displacements in 2024, the highest figure since its records began in 2008, with floods and storms the main causes. Climate change rarely acts alone: it combines with poverty, conflict, population growth and poor governance to influence whether people move. People who cross borders because of climate impacts are often called ‘climate refugees’, but they are not usually covered by the 1951 Refugee Convention, which protects people fleeing persecution, so their legal protection is limited. Some countries, such as small island nations, are planning for possible relocation of whole communities.",
s:[["Sudden and slow","Sudden disasters, such as Cyclone Idai in Mozambique in 2019 or floods in Pakistan in 2022, can displace millions in days. Slow changes, such as long droughts in the Sahel or rising seas in low-lying deltas, push people to move over years."],["Legal gaps","The 1951 Refugee Convention does not cover environmental reasons for leaving. Some agreements, such as the 2023 Falepili Union treaty between Australia and Tuvalu, create special pathways for climate-affected people."],["Planned relocation","Some communities are moving away from danger. Villages in Fiji and Alaska have begun relocating to higher ground because of erosion and flooding."],["Projections","The World Bank has estimated that without strong action, climate change could force up to about 216 million people to move within their countries by 2050."]],
k:[["Causes","Floods, storms, drought, heat, sea-level rise"],["Most movement","Within countries"],["Disaster displacements (2024)","About 45.8 million (record)"],["Data source","Internal Displacement Monitoring Centre"],["Legal protection","Limited; not covered by the 1951 Convention"],["World Bank estimate","Up to 216 million internal migrants by 2050"]],
d:[["2008","IDMC begins tracking disaster displacement"],["2018","Global Compact for Migration mentions climate"],["2021","World Bank Groundswell report"],["2023","Australia–Tuvalu Falepili Union treaty"],["2025","IDMC reports record disaster displacement for 2024"]],
s2:[["Cities","Many climate migrants move to cities, where they may live in informal settlements that themselves face flooding and heat risks."],["Staying put","Some of the most vulnerable people cannot afford to move at all, a situation researchers call ‘trapped populations’."]],
g:[["Climate migration","Movement of people driven partly by climate change"],["Displacement","Being forced to leave home"],["Internal displacement","Being displaced within your own country"],["Refugee","Someone forced to flee across a border"]],
f:["Most climate migration happens within countries.","Disasters caused about 45.8 million displacements in 2024.","Climate refugees are not covered by the 1951 Refugee Convention."],
fun:"Some villages in Fiji have already moved to higher ground because of rising seas."});

ADDART({id:"paygap",t:"The Gender Pay Gap",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Gender pay gap",r:["feminism","inequality","averages","money","fourdayweek"],
p:[["Unequal earnings","On average, women earn less than men."],["Many causes","Job types, hours, career breaks and discrimination."],["Slowly closing","Full gender parity is still over a century away."]],
l:"The gender pay gap is the difference between the average earnings of men and women, usually expressed as a percentage of men’s earnings. Across the world, women on average earn less than men, though the size of the gap varies greatly between countries and industries. The International Labour Organization estimates that, globally, women earn roughly 20 percent less than men on average. The pay gap is not the same as unequal pay for the same job, which is illegal in many countries; it reflects many factors. Women are more likely to work in lower-paid sectors and roles, such as caring and cleaning, and less likely to reach senior, highly paid positions. They are more likely to work part-time and to take career breaks to care for children or relatives, often called the ‘motherhood penalty’, while fathers’ earnings are less affected. Discrimination, both open and hidden, also plays a part. Many countries now require large employers to report their gender pay gaps. The World Economic Forum’s Global Gender Gap Report 2025, which measures economic, educational, health and political gaps, estimated that at the current rate of progress, full gender parity would take about 123 years to reach.",
s:[["Measuring the gap","The pay gap can be calculated using mean or median hourly earnings. Different methods give different figures, so it is important to compare like with like."],["Equal pay laws","Laws requiring equal pay for equal work include the US Equal Pay Act of 1963 and the UK’s Equal Pay Act of 1970. The 1951 ILO Equal Remuneration Convention set an international standard."],["Pay transparency","Since 2017 the UK has required employers with 250 or more staff to publish their gender pay gaps. An EU Pay Transparency Directive, adopted in 2023, must be in national law by June 2026."],["Iceland","Iceland has topped the Global Gender Gap Index for many years. In 2018 it began requiring companies to prove they pay men and women equally for work of equal value."]],
k:[["Definition","Difference in average earnings of men and women"],["Global estimate (ILO)","Women earn about 20% less"],["Causes","Job types, hours, career breaks, discrimination"],["Equal pay for equal work","Required by law in many countries"],["WEF 2025","About 123 years to full parity"],["Top-ranked country","Iceland"]],
d:[["1951","ILO Equal Remuneration Convention"],["1963","US Equal Pay Act"],["1970","UK Equal Pay Act"],["2017","UK pay gap reporting begins"],["2023","EU Pay Transparency Directive adopted"]],
s2:[["Equal Pay Day","Many countries mark Equal Pay Day, a date symbolizing how far into the year women must work to earn what men earned in the previous year."],["Unpaid work","Women do a much larger share of unpaid care and household work worldwide, which affects their paid working hours."]],
g:[["Pay gap","A difference in average pay"],["Equal pay","Same pay for the same or equal work"],["Motherhood penalty","Lower earnings for mothers"],["Transparency","Openness about information such as pay"]],
f:["On average, women earn less than men.","The pay gap has many causes.","Iceland ranks top for gender equality."],
fun:"Equal Pay Day marks how far into the year women must work to match men’s earnings from the year before."});

ADDART({id:"foodbanks",t:"Food Banks",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🥫",w:"Food bank",r:["foodwaste","foodsecurity","inequality","philanthropy","volunteering"],
p:[["Free food for people in need","Collected from donors and given out locally."],["Fighting hunger and waste","Surplus food goes to people instead of landfill."],["Run by volunteers","Charities, churches and communities organize them."]],
l:"A food bank is a charitable organization that collects food from donors and distributes it, free of charge, to people who cannot afford enough to eat, either directly or through local charities, soup kitchens, shelters and schools. Food banks receive donations from supermarkets, farmers, food manufacturers, restaurants and the public, including surplus food that is still safe to eat but would otherwise be thrown away, helping to reduce food waste. Most rely heavily on volunteers to sort, pack and deliver food. The first food bank, St. Mary’s Food Bank, was founded in Phoenix, Arizona, in 1967 by John van Hengel, who saw that grocery stores were throwing out edible food. The idea spread across North America, Europe and later around the world; global networks such as the Global FoodBanking Network now support food banks in dozens of countries. Demand for food banks rose sharply during the 2008 financial crisis, the COVID-19 pandemic and the cost-of-living crisis of the early 2020s. Supporters praise food banks for helping millions of families in difficult times, while many campaigners argue that relying on charity is not a long-term solution to poverty and that governments should address its causes.",
s:[["How they work","Large food banks act like warehouses, collecting and storing food and passing it to local pantries and charities. Some give out food parcels directly, often with a referral from a social worker, doctor or school."],["Food rescue","Many food banks rescue surplus food from farms, factories and shops. France passed a law in 2016 requiring large supermarkets to donate unsold edible food rather than throw it away."],["Community pantries","Community fridges and pantries, where neighbors leave and take food freely, spread in many cities, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Debate","Some experts argue that food banks treat the symptoms rather than the causes of hunger, such as low wages and benefits, and that their growth shows gaps in social safety nets."]],
k:[["Purpose","Free food for people in need"],["Food sources","Shops, farms, manufacturers, public donations"],["First food bank","St. Mary’s, Phoenix, 1967"],["Founder","John van Hengel"],["Run mainly by","Volunteers and charities"],["Also reduces","Food waste"]],
d:[["1967","First food bank opens in Phoenix"],["1979","US national network (now Feeding America) founded"],["2006","Global FoodBanking Network founded"],["2016","France requires supermarkets to donate surplus food"],["2020","Demand surges during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Food drives","Schools and workplaces often hold food drives, collecting canned and dry goods for local food banks."],["Not just food","Many food banks also provide hygiene products, baby supplies and advice on benefits and jobs."]],
g:[["Food bank","A charity that distributes free food"],["Surplus","Extra that is not needed"],["Pantry","A place where food is stored or given out"],["Food insecurity","Not having reliable access to enough food"]],
f:["Food banks give free food to people in need.","The first food bank opened in 1967.","Food banks help reduce food waste."],
fun:"The first food bank began when a volunteer noticed a supermarket throwing away perfectly good food."});

ADDART({id:"organdonation",t:"Organ Donation",c:"Economics and Society",i:"❤️",w:"Organ donation",r:["transplant","kidney","liver","heart","bloodtypes","stemcells"],
p:[["The gift of life","Donated organs can save lives."],["Living and deceased donors","Some organs can be given while alive."],["Opt-in or opt-out","Countries have different consent systems."]],
l:"Organ donation is giving an organ or tissue to help someone whose own organ has failed or been damaged. Organs that can be transplanted include kidneys, the liver, heart, lungs, pancreas and intestines, and tissues include corneas, skin, bone, heart valves and tendons. Most organs come from deceased donors, people who have died, usually after brain death or the stopping of the heart in hospital, whose families or earlier wishes have agreed to donation. One deceased donor can save up to eight lives and help many more. Living donors can give one of their two kidneys or part of their liver, which regrows, usually to a relative or friend, though some donate to strangers. Hundreds of thousands of people around the world are waiting for transplants, and many die before an organ becomes available, so countries try to increase donation. Some use an ‘opt-in’ system, where people register to become donors, while others use ‘opt-out’, or presumed consent, where adults are considered donors unless they say no; families are usually still consulted. Spain has led the world in deceased organ donation for more than 30 years, thanks to a well-organized system of trained transplant coordinators in hospitals.",
s:[["Matching","Donors and recipients are matched by blood type, tissue type and size. Recipients take medicines that suppress the immune system for the rest of their lives so that their body does not reject the new organ."],["The Spanish model","Spain’s National Transplant Organization, created in 1989, placed specialist coordinators in every hospital. In 2024 Spain carried out more than 6,400 transplants, with one of the highest donation rates in the world."],["Opt-out laws","Countries such as Spain, Austria, Wales (from 2015), England (from 2020) and Scotland have opt-out systems. Others, such as the United States and Germany, use opt-in registration."],["Firsts","The first successful kidney transplant was performed in 1954 in Boston between identical twins. Christiaan Barnard performed the first human heart transplant in Cape Town, South Africa, in 1967."]],
k:[["Organs","Kidneys, liver, heart, lungs, pancreas, intestines"],["Tissues","Corneas, skin, bone, valves"],["One deceased donor","Can save up to 8 lives"],["Living donation","A kidney or part of the liver"],["Consent systems","Opt-in or opt-out"],["World leader","Spain"]],
d:[["1954","First successful kidney transplant"],["1967","First human heart transplant"],["1989","Spain’s National Transplant Organization founded"],["2015","Wales introduces opt-out donation"],["2020","England introduces opt-out donation"]],
s2:[["Talk to family","Health services encourage people to tell their families about their donation wishes, because families are usually asked before donation."],["Pig organs","Scientists have transplanted genetically modified pig kidneys and hearts into a few patients since 2022 as experimental treatments to address organ shortages."]],
g:[["Organ donation","Giving an organ to help someone else"],["Transplant","Moving an organ from one person to another"],["Opt-out","Being included unless you say no"],["Rejection","The immune system attacking a transplanted organ"]],
f:["One donor can save up to eight lives.","A living person can donate a kidney.","Spain leads the world in organ donation."],
fun:"The liver can regrow, so a living donor can give part of theirs and both pieces grow back."});
